Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action contemporary drama; naturalistic faces, period-true 1990s-2000s Milwaukee and later Seattle; no stylized illustration.

FADE IN:

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT BUILDING - DAY

A fifth-floor brick walk-up in downtown Milwaukee. Wind pushes trash along the curb. PETER, mid-twenties, lean, plain clothes, brown hair, hauls grocery bags toward the stoop. NICK, older, bigger, scarred chin and left eyebrow, reddish-brown messy hair, steel-worker build in a worn jacket, carries six bags like nothing.

(SOUND: city traffic, distant bus hiss, bags rustling)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. Face like a puzzle held with glue-gun scars. When he walked in, people moved.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - DAY

Cramped living room. MA (OLIVIA), sixties, tired brown eyes, soft housedress, sits near a pill line on the counter. Game-show noise from a small TV. Peter sets bags down. Nick fills the doorway.

(SOUND: TV applause, floorboards creak)

NICK
What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day? You got to keep an eye on her.

PETER
I will. I promise.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Dad took off when I was eight. Nick became the dad. Viking blood, he said. Modern-day warrior. I lived here so Ma wouldn’t fall and nobody know.

EXT. ALLEY BEHIND BUILDING - DAY (FLASH)

Kids’ kickball. A ball smashes through a kitchen window. Young NICK steps up to an angry neighbor while smaller PETER watches.

(SOUND: glass shatter, kids gasp)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He took the blame for Mark Conner’s kick. Paid Ma back sixty bucks somehow. Never told.

INT. MOXIE’S GYM - DAY

Weights clang. A lifter drops a curl bar the second Nick approaches. Nick nods, takes over with a heavier bar. Peter watches from the side, smaller, proud and small at once.

(SOUND: iron plates slam, grunts)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I ain’t gonna curl fifty as a warm-up. That’s okay. Nick stands up for me no matter what.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

Peter locks a bike. SIONNA, mid-twenties, short brown hair, pale skin, light-blue eyes, black V-neck, fish-symbol necklace, jeans, backpack, trips on cement and brushes his shoulder.

(SOUND: bike chain tick, street noise, soft stumble)

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes her shirt. Their eyes hold.

PETER
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna. Irish goddess. River Shannon.

PETER
You want pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. Coffee sometime. Call me.

She writes a number, walks away, glances back over her shoulder. Peter stands frozen.

(SOUND: her footsteps recede, door chime as he enters work)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No girl ever interested in me. Pretty, not cute. Pale like an old English story. It was like I knew her. Intense. Like tides coming in and I didn’t want to move.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma lines pill bottles, glassy-eyed, mumbling.

(SOUND: bottles clink, fridge hum)

MA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta?

PETER
Ma, we don’t go to church. Pills are right in front of you.

She snaps back, embarrassed. Peter’s face tightens.

INT. JOE’S BAR - NIGHT

Packers on TV. Nick slaps Peter’s back. Smoke and pool balls.

(SOUND: crowd roar on TV, cue ball crack, bar chatter)

NICK
What’s goin’ on? You’re hidin’ something.

PETER
Ma was confused. Church. Mrs. Canasta. Empty eyes.

NICK
I knew it. Cooped up. Take her to the store before she turns demented and we pay a nursing home. I don’t have that money.

Peter pays, leaves under Nick’s hard look.

EXT. CITY LIBRARY - NIGHT

Peter ducks in from cold. Old wood floors, chain-hung lights, classic literature stacks. He takes Wuthering Heights, gets a temporary card from the LIBRARIAN.

(SOUND: door whoosh, quiet page rustle, stamp thump)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when it was free here? Heathcliff and Catherine. Reminded me of Sionna. I mixed them in a dream.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College crowd. Sionna at a back table, green hooded top, watery blue eyes. Peter sits, nervous.

(SOUND: espresso machine hiss, low indie music, murmur)

SIONNA
What are you reading?

PETER
Wuthering Heights. Guy and girl grew apart but loved each other.

SIONNA
I know. Ghosts. Doubles. Moors. Love that never changes destroys you.

Peter listens like drowning in light.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something inside woke up. She made me feel smarter. We made a dinner date. Love that kills if you don’t let it go—maybe that was Ma after Dad left. I would never forgive him. Never leave.

EXT. PARK / MONTAGE - DAY

Peter and Sionna walk, museum steps, talk. Quick cuts: yoga studio—Peter on a purple mat, shirt falling, then calm after savasana; library Buddhism shelf; pill organizer on Ma’s table.

(SOUND: birds, museum echo, yoga instructor soft count, pages turning)

SIONNA
Buddhists say everything is a dream that won’t last. Let go. Don’t grasp.

PETER
Maybe the horse dream was a past life. You were the girl.

SIONNA
Some people think dreaming is the real part.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I wasn’t just pizza and Ma’s keeper. For once I mattered.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - NIGHT

Nick stomps. Ma in her chair. Peter in the doorway.

(SOUND: door slam, TV low)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor, you ass. Where were you?

PETER
With someone. A girl. Dates.

NICK
A girl. Priorities, Bro.

Nick slams out. Later—argument over meds container Sionna helped with. Nick slaps Peter’s head. Ear rings.

(SOUND: sharp slap, door crash)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I never fought him before. It bubbled out. Me and Ma and Sionna would be fine without him.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

Ma admitted, high blood pressure, dementia notes. Sionna brings lunch outside. Peter dozes in a chair—dream flash: battlefield, dying man says “Save her,” Nick stabs Peter.

(SOUND: monitor beep, distant PA, Peter gasps awake)

PETER
(whisper)
Only a dream.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - NIGHT

Peter shows Ma the weekly pill box. Nick enters, mocks “nurse Nancy,” sneers at Sionna’s name. Fight escalates. Slap. Nick gone.

(SOUND: plastic box rattle, raised voices, slap, slam)

INT. COMMUNITY COLLEGE CLASSROOM - DAY

Peter among mixed-age students. Later at home: old computer on desk. Sionna helps homework. Kiss on the sidewalk after skateboard kids laugh.

(SOUND: chalk squeak, keyboard clack, distant skate wheels)

SIONNA
I could never love anyone like I love you.

PETER
I could never love anyone the way I love you either.

INT. NICK’S APARTMENT / JOE’S BAR - NIGHT

Nick, BOB, SAMMY smoke and mock Peter’s “perfect girlfriend.” At Joe’s, Nick spots ex LINDSEY with a dark-haired man. Rage builds. Peter steps between.

(SOUND: rock music, pool crack, bottles, crowd hush)

PETER
Leave it, Nick. You don’t know this guy.

NICK
Keep out of it or you’re next.

Nick’s fist draws back. Bouncers rush. Chaos. Peter runs into the night to Sionna’s.

(SOUND: scuffle, AC/DC under roar, Peter’s boots pounding pavement, ragged breath)

INT. SIONNA’S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Peter collapses into her. She holds him.

(SOUND: clock tick, his breathing slows)

SIONNA
No one who cares tries to hit you. You’re becoming bravely compassionate. Don’t let him stop your purpose.

PETER
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
Find the true self. Honor it.

They sleep. Dream: angel-Sionna from lake water pushes giant-Nick back.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - DAY

Months later. Ma laughs at Sionna’s stories. Nick finally meets Sionna over pie. She dismantles his line on women and college with calm facts. Nick’s smile turns snake-thin.

(SOUND: forks on plates, TV murmur)

SIONNA
Diversity helps us learn. You can’t keep a good woman down.

NICK
(to Peter, low)
Take care of Ma.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - DAY

Sionna drives her Civic. Green hills. Toward her parents’ farm—break before summer session. Peter passenger, lighter.

(SOUND: tires on asphalt, open window wind)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Best of times, worst of times. Nick the chameleon took Ma to mass. I should have questioned why.

INT. FIFTH-FLOOR APARTMENT - DAY

September. Empty. No Ma. Answering machine blinks.

(SOUND: machine beep, Peter’s keys hit table)

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olson, Milwaukee County Sheriff. We need to talk regarding your family.

NURSE (V.O.)
Frodo Hospital. Update on your family members.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Stone building 1898. OFFICER DUPUIS, graying, thin mustache, faces pale Peter.

(SOUND: phones, distant holding-cell clang, Peter’s cup shakes)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Last night, intersection Werner and Lowe. Your brother Nicholas serious at Frodo. Your mother Olivia passed at the scene. Toxicology pending. Possible alcohol.

Peter’s world tunnels.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU HALLWAY - DAY

Through glass: Nick’s wild hair, tubes, chest drain, broken leg. Peter turns away.

(SOUND: monitors, soft shoes on tile)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They pulled Ma with the jaws of life. She never knew. Nick killed her. I screamed for him to die.

INT. FUNERAL HOME / CEMETERY - DAY

Modest casket. Few mourners. Dirt hits lid. Headstone: Olivia Anne Olsen. 1933–1998. I have fought the good fight.

(SOUND: muted thunk of earth, quiet sobs, wind in trees)

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Days later. Nick bruised yellow-green, weak. Tears.

(SOUND: oxygen hiss, TV mute click)

NICK
I fucked up. Three Jack and Cokes. Didn’t mean—cops say manslaughter.

PETER
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Peter walks out on Nick’s fury. Powerless giant in a bed.

INT. SIONNA’S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Life thins to work, school, grief. Peter stares. Sionna massages his neck.

(SOUND: soft rain on glass, her quiet voice)

SIONNA
Don’t trip over what’s behind you. This is your life. I love you.

INT. SIONNA’S DUPLEX - DAY

Nick forces entry. Switchblade. Hand on her throat. Demands money, demands she leave Peter.

(SOUND: door thunk, her choked breath, blade snick)

NICK
You’re why I’m going to jail. Break it off. Cash in a week. Or friends visit your parents.

Front door slams open—Peter. Struggle. Blade sinks. Blood. Nick elbows Peter, flees.

(SOUND: body hit wall, wet gasp, running steps, Peter’s 911 shout)

PETER
My brother stabbed her—Sionna McAllister—hurry—

INT. HOSPITAL / MONTAGE - NIGHT TO MONTHS

Pressure on wound saves her. Shoulder, lung. Long recovery. Blank eyes. February talk: she can’t feel herself. Needs space, parents’ farm, therapist. Blame under the love.

(SOUND: monitor beep fades to silence of empty hallway)

SIONNA
As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
She was my guide. She woke me. Then free fall.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - DAY (YEARS LATER)

Gray mist. PETER, mid-thirties now, fuller build, walks Belltown. Tony’s Boy’s Pizza under a medical building. Ferry horn distant. He is owner now—chain growing.

(SOUND: light rain, ferry horn, keyboard busker muffled)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Seven years. Hatred for my brother. Loneliness. Tony’s name on the stores in his honor. I still saw her hair in every crowd.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles. Gratefulness theme. Peter on a mat. Later home: cat BOZO. Dream of a sage and a pizza box—stop judging; all illusion; create reality; forgive.

(SOUND: soft chant, rain outside, cat purr)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had to see Nick. Put it down forever.

INT. PRISON VISITING BOOTH - DAY

Green Bay. Plexiglass. Phone receivers. NICK, graying, thinner, bags under eyes, in greens. Fifteen minutes.

(SOUND: door buzzes, muffled voices, phone plastic scrape)

PETER
You’re an asshole. You killed Ma drunk. You held me down. You drove Sionna away. I forgive you. It sets me free. I still love you for being my brother. Get better. I own a pizza chain in Seattle. I did it.

NICK
(crying)
You’re so right. I’m a huge asshole. You’re a big man. I’m proud of you. President Olson.

Guard ends it. Nick shuffled away. Peter sits with the dead receiver, light.

(SOUND: door locks echo, his long exhale)

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY / AIRPORT - DAY

Peter drives. Hills roll. Boarding queue. Boards. Whisper.

(SOUND: tires, terminal murmur, boarding scan beep)

PETER
(whisper)
That’s me. President Peter Olson.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me. Weight of thirty years gone. Free.

FADE OUT.

THE END
